{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/store/fs.cljc; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/repl/store.cljc | sed -n '210,390p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '285,345p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yin.repl.store.fs\n     2\t  \"Explicit host file operations for the durable index store directory\n     3\t   (docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md, the durable-store design's\n     4\t   host section): an exclusive directory lock held by the one process\n     5\t   that owns the store, and an atomic write-temp, sync, rename\n     6\t   replacement for HEAD. CLJ, Node, and Dart each implement them with\n     7\t   their own primitives; nothing here falls back silently.\n     8\t\n     9\t   Lock ownership is an operating-system fact on the JVM — an exclusive\n    10\t   `FileChannel` lock on `<dir>/lock`, released when the holding process\n    11\t   dies — and on Dart, `RandomAccessFile.lockSync`. Node\n    12\t   has no flock in its core, so the lock is a create-exclusive lock file\n    13\t   carrying the owner's pid: a live owner refuses, and a dead owner's\n    14\t   file is taken over — one contender at a time, under an\n    15\t   exclusive-create takeover marker — so a crashed process never bricks\n    16\t   the directory and two contenders never both own it.\n    17\t\n    18\t   HEAD replacement writes the whole record to a temp file in the same\n    19\t   directory, syncs it, and renames it over HEAD — rename is the\n    20\t   atomicity, so a torn or interrupted write is never observed as HEAD.\n    21\t   The directory itself is then synced where the host can\n    22\t   (`directory-sync-supported?`: Node and the JVM on POSIX systems), and\n    23\t   there a failed sync fails the replacement; a host that cannot sync a\n    24\t   directory at all (Dart's core, Windows) is not a failed sync.\n    25\t\n    26\t   The operating-system locks are per process — a POSIX `fcntl` lock\n    27\t   never refuses its own process — so this process's own holdings are\n    28\t   kept here too, in `held`: a second owner inside the one process is\n    29\t   refused by that record before any second handle on the lock file is\n    30\t   opened.  `held` is process-global mutable state, an explicit\n    31\t   exception to the no-hidden-global-state invariant: it mirrors a fact\n    32\t   the host itself keeps per process (which files this process has\n    33\t   locked), so it can be no narrower than the process, and it holds\n    34\t   nothing but the canonical paths of the directories this process\n    35\t   currently owns.\"\n    36\t  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:io\" :as dart-io]])\n    37\t            [clojure.string :as str]))\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\t(def lock-name\n    41\t  \"The lock file's own name inside the durable store directory. Its\n    42\t   presence is bookkeeping, never authority: the JVM and Dart locks live\n    43\t   on open handles, and Node's file names a pid that is checked for\n    44\t   life.\"\n    45\t  \"lock\")\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\t(defn- refuse-locked\n    49\t  [dir]\n    50\t  (throw (ex-info (str dir \" is locked by another process\")\n    51\t                  {:dir dir})))\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\t(defn- read-text!\n    55\t  \"The file at `path` as text — trimmed, for one-record files read whole —\n    56\t   or nil when absent.\"\n    57\t  [path]\n    58\t  #?(:cljd (let [f (dart-io/File. path)]\n    59\t             (when (.existsSync f)\n    60\t               (str/trim (.readAsStringSync f))))\n    61\t     :clj (let [f (java.io.File. ^String path)]\n    62\t            (when (.exists f) (str/trim (slurp f))))\n    63\t     :cljs (let [fs (js/require \"fs\")]\n    64\t             (when (.existsSync ^js fs path)\n    65\t               (str/trim (.readFileSync ^js fs path \"utf8\"))))))\n    66\t\n    67\t\n    68\t#?(:cljs\n    69\t   (defn- live-pid?\n    70\t     \"True